# --- PRISM ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Appended by setup. Every line is here because PRISM puts a file in your
# repository that something EXECUTES, and an executed file cannot survive a
# line-ending rewrite.

# THE SHEBANG IS THE WHOLE REASON. `core.autocrlf=true` is what several Windows
# git installers set, and it rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. A `#!/bin/sh`
# whose first line ends in \r is not a shebang: the kernel looks for an
# interpreter named `/bin/sh\r` and reports `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`. The
# file most exposed to this is .git/hooks/pre-push -- so the failure lands on
# the one path that cannot be bypassed, at the moment it is meant to be
# protecting something.
#
# PRISM writes these files with newline="\n" itself (lib/render.py's
# write_atomically, and place.py through it). That covers the install. It does
# nothing about the NEXT person to clone the repository, which is what these
# lines are for.
prism                               text eol=lf
gradlew                             text eol=lf
*.sh                                text eol=lf
*.py                                text eol=lf

# Extensionless and executable, so no glob above reaches them.
.prism/verify/prism-doctor          text eol=lf
.prism/adapters/lib/prism-hook      text eol=lf
.prism/adapters/git/pre-push        text eol=lf

# Read by Gradle and rewritten by every promotion. Not executed, so a CRLF here
# breaks nothing -- but it would make the file churn in git between a Windows
# and a macOS contributor, on the one file that records what blocks.
.prism/scope.json                   text eol=lf
.prism/prism.json                   text eol=lf
detekt.yml                          text eol=lf
.editorconfig                       text eol=lf

# The other direction, and it is not symmetry for its own sake: cmd.exe is
# genuinely sensitive to line endings inside a multi-line construct, so a batch
# file normalised to LF can fail in ways that are hard to read.
*.bat                               text eol=crlf
*.cmd                               text eol=crlf

# NOT LISTED, DELIBERATELY: the detekt and ktlint baselines. They are generated
# XML that PRISM rewrites, they are never executed, and `text` on a file a tool
# regenerates wholesale buys nothing. They must still be COMMITTED -- see the
# .gitignore fragment, which says so at more length.
